  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Leucocoprinus ianthinus is a small, delicate mushroom with a distinctive violet or lilac-colored cap and stem, belonging to the Agaricaceae family. This species is commonly found in greenhouses, gardens, and cultivated areas throughout New England, often appearing on rich, composted soils.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Violet-Lilac Coloration",
      "description": "The entire mushroom displays a characteristic violet to lilac hue that fades with age, making it easily distinguishable from other small white or brown mushrooms",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Delicate Structure",
      "description": "The mushroom is notably fragile and small, typically 1-3 cm in diameter, with a thin, hollow stem that breaks easily",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Free Gills",
      "description": "The gills are free from the stem (not attached) and white to pale yellowish, contrasting with the violet cap",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Leucocoprinus ianthinus thrives in moist, organic-rich soils in greenhouses, gardens, potted plants, and composted areas throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "This mushroom is sometimes called the 'mica cap' due to its shiny, translucent appearance, and it frequently appears as a surprise in houseplant pots and indoor gardens where humidity is high.",
